<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">

  <!--
    Installs the Control Center's self-contained single-file exe, its
    Phase 17/18 PowerShell scripts, a Start Menu shortcut, and (Phase
    18) a narrowly-ACL'd %ProgramData% settings folder. Phase 19 added
    real directory selection (WixUI_InstallDir) and a 64-bit-OS launch
    condition.

    Deliberate scope boundary, not an oversight: uninstalling this MSI
    never touches the WSL2 distribution or its data, in either
    direction (no automatic backup, no automatic removal). ADR-0012's
    "remove the application, preserve customer data by default"
    principle is honored here in the safest way available to an
    unattended MSI transaction, since silent/enterprise uninstalls
    (`msiexec /x /quiet`, the common SCCM/Group Policy removal path)
    give no opportunity to confirm a destructive action or wait out a
    real pg_dump-backed backup cycle, so this package simply never
    initiates either. Removing the Intra-Cloud deployment itself is a
    separate, deliberate action through the Control Center or
    Uninstall-IntraCloudDistro.ps1 directly, not a side effect of
    Programs and Features.

    UpgradeCode is fixed forever once this ships for real (it's the
    identity MSI uses to recognize "this is an upgrade of the same
    product," not a new install) — generated once via
    [guid]::NewGuid(), never regenerate it casually.
  -->
  <Package Name="Intra-Cloud Control Center"
           Manufacturer="Intra-Cloud"
           Version="$(var.ProductVersion)"
           UpgradeCode="622B2C0F-8C41-4F9D-8039-76F99791EF6A"
           Language="1033"
           Scope="perMachine">

    <MajorUpgrade DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of Intra-Cloud Control Center is already installed." />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!-- Phase 19: WSL2 requires a 64-bit OS regardless of anything
         Test-Prerequisites.ps1 checks later. A native MSI launch
         condition catches the one prerequisite that's both cheap and
         100% reliable to express this way (VersionNT64 is only set by
         the MSI engine itself on a 64-bit Windows), so a 32-bit machine
         gets a clear message immediately rather than a package that
         silently can't place a 64-bit exe under ProgramFiles64Folder.
         The rest of Test-Prerequisites.ps1's checks (Windows build
         number, virtualization, WSL2 itself) are NOT wired into a
         blocking install-time custom action here. Doing that correctly
         needs the script embedded as MSI Binary data and extracted
         before InstallFiles runs, and this session cannot verify a
         custom action's real runtime behavior without a genuinely
         elevated install attempt (the same UAC wall documented in
         installer/README.md since Phase 16). Left as a known, explicit
         gap rather than shipped unverified; see
         docs/architecture/ROADMAP.md's Phase 19 entry. -->
    <Launch Condition="Installed OR VersionNT64" Message="Intra-Cloud requires a 64-bit version of Windows." />

    <!-- Root-caused during this session's re-verification pass
         (docs/architecture/ROADMAP.md, "Re-verification pass
         (2026-08-27)"): a non-elevated `msiexec /i ... /quiet` install
         attempt left orphaned files under Program Files and a stray
         Windows Installer product-cache entry. Not a custom-action bug;
         this package has none. The actual mechanism: for a
         Scope="perMachine" package, Windows Installer's execute-sequence
         actions (InstallFiles, etc.) run through the Windows Installer
         *service* impersonating SYSTEM, which can write to Program
         Files/HKLM regardless of the calling process's own elevation.
         The "does the caller actually have permission for a
         per-machine install" check (Error 1925) doesn't fire until
         InstallFinalize, by which point files are already staged. When
         that late check fails and rollback runs, this session
         separately reproduced rollback itself hitting access-denied
         (verbose log: "Error in rollback skipped. Return: 5") and not
         fully reversing those already-staged writes.
         `Privileged` is Windows Installer's own built-in property (set
         only when the running install session actually has
         administrator rights for a per-machine install); checking it
         here, in LaunchConditions, rejects a non-elevated attempt
         before InstallValidate/InstallFiles ever run, so nothing is
         ever staged to roll back in the first place.
         Verified directly, end to end, including driving the real
         wizard through UI Automation up to and past clicking "Install":
         a plain double-click (ShellExecute-style launch, no explicit
         "Run as administrator") does NOT itself trigger UAC — the
         wizard opens non-elevated and lets the user browse Welcome/
         License/destination-folder pages, which is harmless since no
         execute-sequence action has run yet. Clicking "Install",
         however, DOES trigger a real UAC consent prompt (confirmed live:
         a genuine `consent.exe` appeared at exactly that step) —
         Windows Installer's own COM elevation for a perMachine package
         requests elevation there, before InstallExecuteSequence runs.
         So the interactive wizard was never actually the vulnerable
         path. The path this condition specifically closes is
         `msiexec /i ... /quiet` (or /qn): quiet mode has no UI at all,
         so Windows Installer has no way to show that same UAC prompt,
         and previously proceeded unelevated via the always-privileged
         Windows Installer service until the too-late Error 1925 check
         at InstallFinalize — by which point RegisterProduct/
         InstallFiles could already have committed. Confirmed by testing
         both paths against this exact build: `/quiet` now fails at
         LaunchConditions (Return value 3, exit 1603) with zero new
         files or registry state; the interactive wizard's UAC prompt,
         when declined, likewise leaves zero new state. -->
    <Launch Condition="Installed OR Privileged" Message="Intra-Cloud Control Center requires administrator privileges to install. Right-click the installer and choose 'Run as administrator', or if installing silently, run msiexec from an elevated (Run as administrator) command prompt." />

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Intra-Cloud">
        <Directory Id="ControlCenterFolder" Name="Control Center">
          <Directory Id="ScriptsFolder" Name="scripts" />
        </Directory>
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Directory Id="ApplicationProgramsFolder" Name="Intra-Cloud" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <!-- Phase 18: the Control Center runs unelevated by design (only the
         out-of-scope-for-Phase-18 import/uninstall actions would ever
         relaunch elevated), so it needs a %ProgramData% location it can
         actually write its own settings file into. Scoped to this one
         subdirectory, not the whole IntraCloud tree, so the WSL/VHDX
         storage under %ProgramData%\IntraCloud\wsl (Import-IntraCloudDistro.ps1's
         default InstallPath) stays exactly as protected as it is today. -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="CommonAppDataFolder">
      <Directory Id="IntraCloudProgramData" Name="IntraCloud">
        <Directory Id="ControlCenterDataFolder" Name="ControlCenter" />
      </Directory>
    </StandardDirectory>

    <ComponentGroup Id="ControlCenterComponents" Directory="ControlCenterFolder">
      <Component Id="ControlCenterExe" Guid="6E4B2B4E-6C2C-4E1B-9B1A-2F5B7B6E1A11">
        <File Id="ControlCenterExeFile"
              Source="$(var.ControlCenterPublishDir)IntraCloudControlCenter.exe"
              KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!-- The Phase 17 lifecycle scripts the Control Center invokes as a
         subprocess (Services/ScriptRunner.cs) — copied into
         ControlCenterPublishDir\scripts\ by the .csproj's own Content
         item, so this harvests the exact same files the published exe
         ships next to in a dev run, not a second copy that could drift. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="ScriptsComponents" Directory="ScriptsFolder">
      <Files Include="$(var.ControlCenterPublishDir)scripts\*.ps1" />
    </ComponentGroup>

    <Component Id="StartMenuShortcut" Directory="ApplicationProgramsFolder" Guid="9A3E6C2D-1B4F-4C8A-8E2D-5A7C3B9F2E44">
      <Shortcut Id="ControlCenterStartMenuShortcut"
                Name="Intra-Cloud Control Center"
                Description="Manage your Intra-Cloud installation"
                Target="[ControlCenterFolder]IntraCloudControlCenter.exe"
                WorkingDirectory="ControlCenterFolder" />
      <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveApplicationProgramsFolder" Directory="ApplicationProgramsFolder" On="uninstall" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                      Key="Software\Intra-Cloud\ControlCenter"
                      Name="installed"
                      Type="integer"
                      Value="1"
                      KeyPath="yes" />
    </Component>

    <!-- Users (BUILTIN), not Everyone or Authenticated Users: the
         narrowest built-in group that still covers every standard local
         account the Control Center would run as. GenericRead/Write/
         Execute + Delete approximates NTFS "Modify" without granting
         ChangePermission/TakeOwnership (that combination stays Full
         Control, deliberately withheld here). -->
    <Component Id="ControlCenterDataFolder" Directory="ControlCenterDataFolder" Guid="B4C1E9A7-3D2F-4A6E-9C8B-1E5F7A2D6C90">
      <CreateFolder>
        <util:PermissionEx User="Users" GenericRead="yes" GenericWrite="yes" GenericExecute="yes" Delete="yes" />
      </CreateFolder>
      <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveControlCenterDataFolder" Directory="ControlCenterDataFolder" On="uninstall" />
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                      Key="Software\Intra-Cloud\ControlCenter"
                      Name="DataFolderProvisioned"
                      Type="integer"
                      Value="1"
                      KeyPath="yes" />
    </Component>

    <Feature Id="ControlCenterFeature" Title="Intra-Cloud Control Center" Level="1">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ControlCenterComponents" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ScriptsComponents" />
      <ComponentRef Id="StartMenuShortcut" />
      <ComponentRef Id="ControlCenterDataFolder" />
    </Feature>

    <!-- Phase 19: WixUI_InstallDir, not WixUI_Minimal. Adds the real
         directory-selection dialog Section 9 of the engineering brief
         calls for. WIXUI_INSTALLDIR must name the top-level directory
         the browse dialog actually controls; pointing it at
         ControlCenterFolder directly (rather than the INSTALLFOLDER
         parent) would let a user browse to change "Intra-Cloud\Control
         Center" independently of "Intra-Cloud", which isn't a
         meaningful choice here. INSTALLFOLDER is the one directory
         users should be choosing. -->
    <Property Id="WIXUI_INSTALLDIR" Value="INSTALLFOLDER" />
    <ui:WixUI Id="WixUI_InstallDir" />
    <!-- Relative to this .wxs file's own project directory
         (installer/wix/), not an MSBuild property — .wxs files don't
         process $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)-style MSBuild property
         functions, only WiX's own $(var.X) preprocessor variables and
         plain paths. -->
    <WixVariable Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="..\assets\License.rtf" />

  </Package>
</Wix>
